/ddy` need all four pixels in a 2x2 quad to evaluate up to that point with the same control flow. If you have `discard` (or `clip()`) in a divergent branch before the derivative call, AMD and NVIDIA handle the now-dead lanes differently. Move all derivatives BEFORE any conditional discard:

```hlsl
// BAD
if (alpha < 0.5) discard;
float3 n = normalFromDDX(viewPos);  // helper lanes may be wrong on AMD

// GOOD
float3 n = normalFromDDX(viewPos);  // all lanes alive
if (alpha < 0.5) discard;
```

**3. Undefined arithmetic at silhouette edges.** Even without NaN, depth ddx/ddy across a silhouette gives you nonsense — you're sampling the gradient between the foreground object and the background, which has no physical meaning. The fix is to use the analytic normal (from a G-buffer or vertex normal) at silhouette edges, or accept the artifact. You can detect silhouettes by checking if `length(ddx(viewPos))` or `length(ddy(viewPos))` exceeds a threshold.

**Diagnostic workflow:** RenderDoc on the AMD machine. Capture a frame, click a black pixel in the affected area, open the pixel-history view, run the pixel shader debugger. You'll see intermediate values per-instruction. NaN shows up as `nan` literally; you can trace back to which op produced it. This takes 5-10 minutes and answers the question definitively.

One more question: are you compiling with FXC or DXC? If DXC, are you targeting SM 6.0+? AMD's driver compiler can take quite different paths depending on the input bytecode.

