We have **body position:fixed** + **blanket event suppression** + **bypassed dialog management** + **viewport-unit sizing** all active simultaneously. Each one individually might be fine, but together they create a stack where iOS Safari can't do what it naturally wants to do with the keyboard.

The blog post's own conclusion — "sometimes you want a library that gives you primitives and gets out of the way" — applies here too. We chose Base UI to get out of the way, but then added back multiple layers of manual control that are collectively doing what Radix's RemoveScroll did: fighting the platform.

## The simplest path forward

Replace the body scroll lock with the CSS-only approach. This removes the biggest interference layer. The touch-action coverage on the overlay + panel overscroll-contain already handle scroll prevention. The event suppression and dialog management can stay (they serve other purposes like click-to-close and preventing accidental closures).

```tsx
// Instead of position:fixed on body:
useEffect(() => {
  if (isActuallyModal) return;
  document.documentElement.classList.add('lightbox-open');
  return () => document.documentElement.classList.remove('lightbox-open');
}, [isActuallyModal]);
```

